.TH git-pull-all "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Git Command"
.SH NAME
git-pull-all \- update every local branch from its upstream branch, whichever branch is checked out
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git pull-all
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBgit pull-all\fR command brings every local branch that has a remote upstream branch up to date,
without checking any of them out. On the branch \fBother\fR, it updates the local \fBmaster\fR from
origin/master and the local \fBother\fR from origin/other.
.IP 1. 4
\fBgit fetch \-\-all \-\-prune\fR: fetches every remote.
.IP 2. 4
For each local branch, printing the branch and the command first:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
the checked out branch: \fBgit merge \-\-ff\-only @{upstream}\fR, so the working tree is updated too;
.IP \[bu] 2
any other branch: \fBgit fetch\fR \fIREMOTE REMOTE_BRANCH\fR:\fIBRANCH\fR, which moves the branch
without touching the working tree;
.IP \[bu] 2
a branch without a remote upstream branch is skipped.
.RE
.PP
Only fast-forwards are made: a branch that has commits of its own, not on its upstream branch, is left as
it is and reported by Git. Then rebase or merge it by hand. When one branch fails, the others are still
updated and the command exits with status 1.
.PP
Git runs it as a subcommand because the executable \fBgit-pull-all\fR is in the \fBPATH\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.B git pull-all
.PP
On the branch other, it prints after the fetch:
.PP
.nf
master: git fetch origin refs/heads/master:refs/heads/master
other: git merge \-\-ff\-only origin/other
.fi
